 **Parameters:**
-*   **First level resolution, Second level resolution:** These control the final resolution and must be adjusted to the sparsity of the input model while respecting GPU memory limits [S1].
-*   **Sample number:** Affects rendering quality and memory usage; high resolution combined with high samples can lead to out-of-memory errors [S1].
+*   **Target Coverage:** The screen-space size (in pixels) that corresponds to the highest resolution level (LOD 0). Not given in the sources [S1].
+*   **Number of Levels:** Determined by the user based on GPU memory and input sparsity [S1].
 
-**Edge Cases:**
-*   **Out of Memory:** Combining elevated sample numbers with high resolutions can exceed GPU memory [S1].
-*   **Microgeometry:** Vegetation and hair are difficult to represent accurately with standard LOD; this method is specifically noted for handling these cases [S1].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Out of Memory:** Combining high resolution with a high number of samples can lead to out-of-memory errors. Users must balance resolution and sample count [S1].
+*   **Degenerate Input:** If the projected width is extremely small (very distant), the method should select the lowest LOD level. If extremely large (very close), it should select the highest. Clamping is necessary [G].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** The selection is $O(1)$ per voxel/anchor after projection. Loading multiple volumes increases memory usage linearly with the number of LoD levels [S1].
-*   **Performance:** The method allows simultaneous loading of multiple LoD volumes [S1]. Performance is constrained by GPU memory when high resolutions and sample counts are combined [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Effective for microgeometry (vegetation, hair) where distance-based methods may fail to capture screen-space detail [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of resolution parameters to balance quality and memory [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** LOD selection is $O(1)$ per voxel/sample, involving basic arithmetic and a logarithmic lookup.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires loading all LOD levels into memory simultaneously, which can be significant for large volumes [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** View-dependent and accurate for microgeometry (vegetation, hair) where distance alone is insufficient [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Memory usage scales with the number of LOD levels. Requires pre-computation of all LODs [S1].
 
 ## Background
-This method is used in the context of fast voxelization and level of detail for microgeometry rendering, specifically for rendering vegetation and hair models [S1]. It builds on the concept of PathTracer MipMap selection [S1].
+This method is used in the context of fast voxelization and level of detail for microgeometry rendering, specifically for rendering vegetation and hair models which are difficult to represent accurately with static LOD [S1]. It adapts the Mipmap selection strategy from path tracing to volume rendering [S1].
 
